<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tālivaldis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1215">1215</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Talivaldis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tālivaldis (died 1215) was the ruler of Tālava, a Latgalian principality in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, known from the chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia</a>. Formerly a tributary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, he accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> baptism with his sons around 1208 and allied with the German crusaders against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>. In 1215 Estonian raiders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sakala</a> captured him and roasted him to death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Thalibaldus de Tolowa, Tālivaldis was a Latgalian elder whose date of birth is unknown. His home district was the area around Trikāta, where he had his castle. He first appears in 1208, fighting against the Saccalians and Ugandians as an ally of the Latgalian dukes Rūsiņš and Varidots, at a time when he was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Pskov Republic.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-crusades">Northern Crusades</a> began, Tālava lay within the spheres of influence of both Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Albert of Riga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>. Tālivaldis chose Albert, hoping that the Germans would help the Tālavians resist pressure from the Russians, and as Albert's vassal he joined the crusaders' wars against the Estonian tribes. In 1211 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> raided Tālava in revenge and pillaged the country around Trikāta. In 1212 he gave no support to the Autīne revolt, a joint Latgalian and Livonian revolt against the crusaders. In 1213 invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> captured him in his castle at Trikāta, but he escaped while being taken back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> as a prisoner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bishop Albert and the crusaders had no interest in keeping an independent Latgalian state, and the war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> between the Tālavians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> served the German occupiers. In 1215 Tālivaldis was killed by Estonian raiders, who according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-chronicle-of-henry">Livonian Chronicle of Henry</a> burnt him alive. His sons Rameka and Drivinalds soon took revenge with a raid that plundered the raiders' land severely. After his death his lands were divided between the crusaders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Albert of Riga</a>, and his sons became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of Albert and adopted Roman Catholicism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tālivaldis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It appears in Henry of Livonia's chronicle in the early 13th century, when it paid tribute to Pskov and was ruled by the elder <b>Tālivaldis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/talava">Tālava</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Trikāta Parish, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=57.55,25.7&amp;year=1205" aria-label="Trikāta Parish on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5101 216 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Tālivaldis"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5551 486h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5551 486h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=57.55,25.7&amp;year=1205">What was here in 1205? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
